{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"The failed-write repeat ran clean and reproduced the first run where it matters. In the commit phase the added server's configuration write failed, the leader's latest configuration index stood at 3 while its last stored log index was 2, the phantom configuration carried three voters, and a client command then committed at index 4 and applied even though the other committed voter's log stayed at index 2 without that entry. After the restart the node reported the two-server committed configuration without the added server, with its commit index reset to zero and a last log index of 4, so the phantom stayed process-local while the entry committed under it remains stored with no commit index covering it. That gives each of the account's decisive observations a second execution: the failed change installed in memory, the lone commit the committed configuration does not authorise, and the non-durability of the phantom. I appended the parsed events for both phases to the rationale, added the receipt to the execution set beside the earlier one, and extended the joint history to record that it is a repeat from retained inputs. All five counterevidence items, seven limitations, both knowledge uses at revision 7 and the empty answers are carried forward unchanged, and the conclusion stays violated with the scoped defect asserted.\"}"}}
